WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:26.440
- Hi everyone, welcome
to our daily Homeroom.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.420
For those of y'all who are
wondering what this is,

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.320
this is something that we
started many months ago.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.540
It was really when all of us had to be

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.450
socially distanced around COVID,

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.680
but it's really evolved into, you know,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.150
an outlet to having
interesting conversations

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.000
and for us to interact with all of y'all.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.370
As we go to the conversation,

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.850
I'm very excited about our guest today.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.030
Feel free to ask questions
on Facebook and YouTube

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:46.863
in the message boards.

00:00:46.863 --> 00:00:48.300
I have team members who are looking at it

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.520
and we will try to surface as
many questions as possible.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.610
But before we begin our conversation,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.670
I will give my standard
announcement reminder to everyone

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.570
that Khan Academy is a not-for-profit,

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.720
that we exist because of
philanthropic donations

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.130
from folks like yourself.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.140
If you're in a position to do so,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.380
please think about donating.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.637
A special shout out to several
corporations that stepped up,

00:01:08.637 --> 00:01:10.560
especially as the COVID
crisis was ramping up

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.560
in our servers, our traffic
was 250%, 300% of normal,

00:01:16.064 --> 00:01:17.540
registrations went through the roof,

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.550
we're trying to accelerate
a bunch of programs.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.620
Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:24.390
AT&amp;T, Fastly and Novartis.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.870
But I will remind everyone,
including any corporations

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:29.060
out there listening, that we
continue to run into deficit,

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.730
so if you in a position
to help out at any scale,

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.250
please think about it.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:38.790
With that I am super excited
of introducing our guest

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.760
who is someone that I've always admired,

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.710
and I'd like to believe
have become a good friend

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:47.740
over the last several
years, Francis Ford Coppola.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.253
Francis, thanks so much for joining us.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.473
- My pleasure to join you.

00:01:54.054 --> 00:01:55.423
Thank you.
- So a good place,

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.960
a good place to start is, you
know, when we scheduled this,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.740
the world was already in
kind of the COVID crisis and,

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.960
you know, I'd thought we
were going to talk a lot,

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.180
and I think we will talk
a lot about learning,

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.946
and especially learning
in times of crisis,

00:02:09.946 --> 00:02:10.779
and what is this doing.

00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:13.720
But obviously there's been
layered on top of COVID

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.300
other things in the world, you know.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:21.300
We have the protests and around
the killing of George Floyd,

00:02:22.328 --> 00:02:25.120
around not just the country, the world.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.110
I would love your view as a
storyteller, as a filmmaker,

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.360
as an observer of the human condition,

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.590
you know, what's your lens
of what you're seeing now?

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.743
And you've also seen a lot
in your lifetime, you know.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.800
Does this have parallels
with other things you've seen

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.580
or is this different in certain ways?

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:46.047
- Well I must say, you
know, I was born in 1939,

00:02:46.047 --> 00:02:50.690
so to me the big event as I was
growing up, as I was a child

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.143
of four and five and
six, was World War II.

00:02:55.518 --> 00:02:58.950
So I was born at a time when
the world was going through

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.370
one of the most terrible
of all bad things,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.173
which is a world war,

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:11.317
and of course, I was
blessed being in America

00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:13.150
and that we were never attacked

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.940
as were our fellows all over the world.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.720
So this, I would say that
this present pandemic

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.720
is the most serious crisis
that I have personally,

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.033
as a more understanding
adult lived through.

00:03:32.461 --> 00:03:34.861
and, you know, my perspective
is that, you know,

00:03:36.034 --> 00:03:40.934
the human species as always
it's always easy to say,

00:03:40.934 --> 00:03:45.010
well, people are selfish
or there's part of them

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.340
that's bad and it always has been,

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.415
but I don't believe that to be the case.

00:03:51.415 --> 00:03:54.920
I believe the story of
humanity, which has been

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.780
certainly going on 50,000 years,

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:03.100
recorded history is only
maybe 7,000 years old,

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.120
but there have been, the
human species is a late comer

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.930
to the family of creatures
that inhabit the earth,

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.670
and we have changed quite
a bit over that time.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:20.370
And usually there is some
tremendous influence,

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.500
some innovation or some event that causes

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.036
a re-adjustment of what life is like.

00:04:28.036 --> 00:04:32.090
It goes way back to
the time of agriculture

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:35.510
when human beings began
to cultivate agriculture

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:41.210
for the first time and suddenly
land became a priority,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.770
so there was landowners,

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.130
and then with landowners
there were the people

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:49.086
who worked on the land and
that evolved into feudalism.

00:04:49.086 --> 00:04:52.990
In each face phase of human
development there was always

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.000
a reason why things changed all the way up

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.720
into, say, modern time with
the Industrial Revolution.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:03.000
It was the learning how
to make force engines,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.310
steam engines and
ultimately mass production,

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.680
and that changed everything.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.560
So this idea to say that
human beings are a certain way

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:18.450
for me is not true, it
depends on what is going

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:23.450
to influence, what happens,

00:05:24.037 --> 00:05:27.000
and therefore we can imagine a time when

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.790
there are other priorities
changed by other things.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:34.790
Our human ingenuity is
constantly inventing something

00:05:35.796 --> 00:05:38.670
that changes the science called economics,

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:40.860
which everyone thinks, "Well,
this is the way it is."

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.650
It's not the way it,
is it's ever-changing.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.180
And so I can imagine definitely

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.780
even out of this crisis I see change.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.707
I don't hear people talking so much about,

00:05:53.707 --> 00:05:58.120
"Oh, I'm worth this much money"
or "I earn that much money."

00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:59.367
I hear them talking about,

00:05:59.367 --> 00:06:03.170
"Oh, my family is okay,
my family is safe."

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:08.170
So that I think that there
has been some positive things

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:11.693
we're learning from this crisis together.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.030
- Absolutely.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:15.830
And and what's your sense, you know,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.570
just as an observer in our country

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:21.410
and maybe around the world,

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.260
but especially in our
country around, you know,

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:25.600
what's around race issues,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.570
I think we'd like to believe
that there's been progress,

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.750
but there's also, you know, it feels like

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:31.040
every now and then we get reminded,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.820
as we've been in the last,
not just last several weeks,

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.090
but last several years,

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:37.780
that, you know, the
things are still happening

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.033
that we thought only
happened 50 years ago.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:42.950
Where do you think we
are in that narrative?

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:46.970
- Yeah, I feel that, you
know, we are all immigrants.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:50.624
There is not an American,
there is not anyone living

00:06:50.624 --> 00:06:52.110
on this continent who is not an immigrant,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:56.860
even the Native Americans came
here maybe 15,000 years ago.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:59.840
No, there were no human beings in any part

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.900
of what we call the New World.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:06.647
So to refer to them as,
"Oh, they're new here,

00:07:06.647 --> 00:07:08.860
"they're not really born here,"

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.860
I heard today on television a
lady berating some young lady

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:18.370
of "Where were you from,
well, this isn't your home."

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.654
The truth of the matter is,

00:07:20.654 --> 00:07:22.770
everyone in America is an immigrant

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.630
and the immigrants have been a source

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.090
of tremendous benefit beyond calculation.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.460
That's when one of the
things that gives us such

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:33.093
vitality and innovation.

00:07:34.484 --> 00:07:38.354
And certainly African
Americans who came here,

00:07:38.354 --> 00:07:40.690
it wasn't even their will to come here,

00:07:40.690 --> 00:07:41.893
they were brought here.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:47.137
African Americans are, you
know, I'm an Italian American,

00:07:47.137 --> 00:07:49.090
and there are Chinese Americans,

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.090
there are African Americans.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:56.090
And African Americans have
struggled unfairly against

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.970
of the prejudices that grew up around them

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.620
during 300 years ago.

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:07.620
And when you think about it,
it will take a big effort

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:12.160
to change the four areas
that is troublesome.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.550
One is of course the
criminal justice system,

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:19.770
two is the equal opportunity
for equal education,

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:23.750
three is, of course, the
health care and the ability

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:26.842
to be healthy, and four, in my opinion,

00:08:26.842 --> 00:08:28.290
is childcare and nutrition,

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:31.420
because these children
have to have good nutrition

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:34.970
so they can learn and
they can blossom and be

00:08:37.377 --> 00:08:42.050
the contributors to our society
that we know is possible.

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:44.171
And you say well to do that,

00:08:44.171 --> 00:08:48.283
to really attack those four areas,

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.370
criminal justice is an enormous job

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.210
and health is an enormous job,

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:56.640
and it's gonna cost a great deal of money.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.040
But that money has been earned,

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:02.070
because the African American contribution

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.170
to American culture over
the last three years

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:08.750
has earned trillions of dollars in revenue

00:09:11.002 --> 00:09:15.240
from the selling of that
culture which they created.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:20.160
Jazz, and literature, and
dance, and so many things

00:09:21.016 --> 00:09:24.163
that have enriched our country.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:28.480
Certainly a percentage
of that as a royalty,

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.480
is worth spending the needed
money that we have to do

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:39.510
to revolutionize those
areas of criminal justice,

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:43.130
health, education, and
childcare, and nutrition.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:46.310
The money was earned by
what they contributed

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:49.740
and they're entitled to
that money to be spent

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.563
in those areas as a royalty.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.120
- Right, it's a fascinating idea.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.410
Money, as you mentioned,
contributed over, you know,

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:59.920
300 years, many, many generations.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.620
You know one of the things
that I think we like to do

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.830
on this Homeroom is for any guest,

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.220
'cause we have a lot of
young people watching.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:10.180
They're at the stage of their life

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:11.630
where they're trying to
figure out what to do,

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:13.050
they have some things that
they're excited about,

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.150
some things that they might
be feeling less secure about,

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.750
and when they see a Francis Ford Coppola,

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:20.780
who's, I mean I don't
think I'm saying anything

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.410
that anyone would disagree
with, a filmmaking legend.

00:10:23.410 --> 00:10:25.230
You know they say, well, how did he become

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:26.403
Francis Ford Coppola?

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.320
So maybe tell us a little
bit about your own journey,

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.670
because I think especially
something like filmmaking,

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:33.830
you know, you can go to film school,

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:36.560
but it doesn't seem as
obvious of a career track,

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.440
as, you know, going to
med school or law school.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.920
How did you become that and
what gave you the confidence,

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:43.190
and did you know, how
did you know that you had

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:44.200
some of the creative gifts?

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.577
You know, Ernie H.Town from YouTube says,

00:10:46.577 --> 00:10:49.010
"Thank you, Mr. Coppola, for
the greatest cinema ever."

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:51.880
And I think a lot of people
would agree with that.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:52.970
So tell us a little
bit about that journey,

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:55.108
especially when you were young.

00:10:55.108 --> 00:10:57.510
- You know, talent is a strange thing.

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.470
And there's two kinds
of talent you can have.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.063
There's that god-given
talent that, you know,

00:11:03.063 --> 00:11:04.540
that we all know those kids,

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:06.500
they can just draw beautiful pictures

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:08.380
and they could and
there's no effort to them.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.280
Or they can sing, or they can
dance, or they've just been

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:13.370
given the gift.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:17.690
The other kind of talent that
really you have to work for.

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:22.140
And one of the beauties of
all of these fields is that

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:26.165
even if you didn't get, I did
not get that god-given gift.

00:11:26.165 --> 00:11:30.670
I would say the filmmakers
like, well, Steven Spielberg

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:34.310
clearly just has the knack
and that just was given

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:37.220
the talent to do it, plus
a lot of work in addition.

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:39.810
And there were great
directors like William Wyler,

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:42.700
who happened to be the nephew of the owner

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:47.110
of Columbia Pictures and
got the chance to do it,

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:48.830
but he had just a gift.

00:11:48.830 --> 00:11:52.423
In my case it was, I saw
a talent all around me,

00:11:53.261 --> 00:11:54.973
my father was a talented musician.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:12:00.160
But I didn't feel I was
given any special anything,

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.760
except that I had enthusiasm,
and I had a good imagination,

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:08.270
and I worked hard, and I
just tried, and I tried,

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:11.270
and I tried to write little
plays when I was a kid,

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:14.130
and I knew they were
awful, but I didn't stop.

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:18.113
So whether you get that
god-given gift or not,

00:12:19.302 --> 00:12:21.880
applying yourself, and working hard,

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:25.260
and having a dream that one
day you'll be able to do

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:27.820
the thing you love, it does work.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:30.870
I have seen actors who
I remember from college

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:34.356
were terrible, but they never
gave up and they kept trying,

00:12:34.356 --> 00:12:39.356
and years later they started
to be actually brilliant.

00:12:42.139 --> 00:12:45.710
So you can either work your
way to what your dream is

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:47.630
or you get the natural gift,

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:52.050
which sometimes is not
even as good as those of us

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:56.600
who have to just keep working at it.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:59.600
And certainly that's true of
writing or any creative thing.

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:03.960
So I was a kid who was
extremely good at science

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:07.660
and I knew all, I understood electricity

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:09.260
and I could make little gadgets,

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:12.980
I could make little electric
motors or do things like that.

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:15.440
And I wanted to be a nuclear physicist,

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:19.047
but I had flunked algebra
twice and my father said,

00:13:19.047 --> 00:13:21.305
"You can't a nuclear
physicist if you're gonna

00:13:21.305 --> 00:13:26.305
flunk algebra," and I say
my mind has difficulty,

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:31.940
unfortunately I looked at the
Khan Academy algebra course,

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:35.250
and at my own pace I was
able to understand it.

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:37.310
I have more of a spatial mind,

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:40.993
so I won the geometry medal in school.

00:13:42.248 --> 00:13:44.410
Because my mind thinks as whole pictures,

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:47.330
doesn't think as a linear thing.

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:50.840
So every kid has the things
they're good at naturally

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:52.440
and the things they're less good at,

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.980
And the trick is to identify
what you're good at,

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:59.330
and often it's what you love.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:02.580
So I loved science, I
wanted to be around science

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:06.550
and technology, and unfortunately
I was taken out of school

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:09.240
for reasons of my father's moving around

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:11.990
and I went to about, I
don't know, maybe 20 schools

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:16.980
before college, and I would
always wander as a new student,

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.010
I had no friends and I knew no one,

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:21.220
and I wasn't doing well in school.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:23.780
So I would wander, the
teacher wouldn't even care

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:26.110
if I said, "Oh, I have
to do a special project."

00:14:26.110 --> 00:14:28.420
I would always wander to
the theater department,

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:31.340
because they always
needed boys at that time

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:34.510
who would climb up on the
stage and hook up the lights.

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:37.250
And from up there with
the lights I would watch

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.497
and, of course, I wanted to
go to the theater department

00:14:39.497 --> 00:14:42.010
because that's where the
girls were, you know.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:43.320
The girls were either in
the theater department

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.610
or they were at the bar
on the football team.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:49.053
Well, I was sure not gonna
meet them playing football,

00:14:50.304 --> 00:14:53.270
so I went, they were at least
at the theater department,

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:55.900
and I wanted to meet one if I could,

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:57.563
or even get to know one.

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.920
So I was there hanging
the lights of the theater,

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:05.200
and I would see the teachers
directing the actors,

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.210
and I thought, "Well, I could do that."

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:11.660
And so that was really the
reason I became a theater major

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:13.970
and ultimately, when I did go to college,

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:17.740
I was a theater student and
that's where I sort of blossomed

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:20.390
because I was doing something
that I really loved,

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:22.210
which was to do plays.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:25.470
And then from that ultimately
the transition came

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:27.400
and I went to film school.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:29.870
I could tell that story
if you're interested

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:31.500
in why I chose film school rather than,

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.440
I was gonna go to the Yale Drama School

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:35.918
for my master's degree,
but something happened

00:15:35.918 --> 00:15:38.453
and I went to film school instead.

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:41.850
- Well now you've, I have to ask you.

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:43.940
Why did you go to, I'm curious,

00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:45.558
why did you go to film school?

00:15:45.558 --> 00:15:48.810
- I was very poor, I
had absolutely no money,

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:53.810
and just to eat was like a
major, to have a lunch was,

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:59.070
you know, a major thing,
and so I would hang around

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:01.350
where they were building the new theater

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:04.600
because I knew I was gonna get
to do plays in that theater.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:07.727
And there was a little
theater and there was a sign

00:16:07.727 --> 00:16:10.150
that said today only four
o'clock we're showing

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:13.440
Sergei Eisenstein's "Ten
Days That Shook the World,"

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:18.270
which I knew he was a
Russian silent film director

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.929
and so I had nothing to
do and I went in there,

00:16:21.929 --> 00:16:25.030
and there were maybe four
people in that little theater.

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:28.200
And I saw this silent movie by Eisenstein,

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:32.410
and I couldn't believe my
eyes, you see this this stuff,

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:36.115
and even though there
was no music or anything,

00:16:36.115 --> 00:16:37.550
it was just a silent film,
but you could hear it,

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:40.160
you could hear the crowds, you
could hear the machine guns,

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:43.849
you could hear everything
because of the way it was edited.

00:16:43.849 --> 00:16:47.280
It was the first time that I
saw what this magical ability

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.450
that film has which we call montage,

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:52.100
where you take a shot and put
it together with another shot

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:54.620
and make something magical.

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:56.617
And when I came out of
that theater I said,

00:16:56.617 --> 00:16:58.857
"I'm not going to the Yale Drama School.

00:16:58.857 --> 00:17:01.430
"I'm gonna be a film
director like Eisenstein."

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:04.929
And I decided to go to
the UCLA Film School

00:17:04.929 --> 00:17:07.030
and my whole life was changed.

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:10.090
- And that relates to a
question from YouTube,

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:13.557
UWU says, "How can you know
not to be too ambitious

00:17:13.557 --> 00:17:14.810
"with your dreams in life?"

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:17.198
Was there any point
where you were thinking

00:17:17.198 --> 00:17:18.740
maybe I'm, you know, filmmaking
isn't a guaranteed job,

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:20.420
how am I going to pay the bills?

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:22.210
Will I even be able to make films?

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.080
How did you know you
weren't being too ambitious?

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:26.340
- Yeah, I had no money,

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:30.690
my father was mad at me because
he had at one point sent me

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.250
to military school which
I didn't like at all

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:35.390
and I ran away, and he had to pay for it,

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:36.460
even though I had run away.

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:39.540
So he didn't wanna support me in college.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:42.380
I had no money, I knew no
one in the film business.

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:45.180
I didn't have a family connection.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.310
I did have the blessing
of a creative family.

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:51.150
My father was a great
musician and I had uncles

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:55.310
who were in music, so
I had seen in theater

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:56.840
that kind of stuff.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.240
But, you know, an answer
to that young person,

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.350
you can't be too ambitious.

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:05.440
You are given this unique
opportunity to live

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:09.260
and you should have the
most wild wonderful ambition

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:12.166
you can dream of, because
anything is possible.

00:18:12.166 --> 00:18:16.680
I'm a good example of that because I was

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:20.120
as poor as you can be.

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:22.990
I remember, I had 25
cents for a breakfast,

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:27.670
I had 40 cents for lunch
and I could sometimes have

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:30.310
for dinner a Kraft
macaroni and cheese dinner,

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:32.580
which in those days cost about 20 cents,

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:34.480
so I was really poor.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:37.950
And I went to the most
wonderful film school,

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:41.250
which was in the University
of California, UCLA,

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:45.070
which was, you know, I
mean tuition was like $40

00:18:45.070 --> 00:18:48.750
a semester or something, which
was a blessing of California

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:53.750
at that time, and there I
was the least possible person

00:18:54.184 --> 00:18:56.980
to get even in the movie business.

00:18:56.980 --> 00:18:59.760
And part of me dreamed, of course,

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.650
that I could do it, but
the other part thought,

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:06.280
well, maybe I can become an
assistant director or something.

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:09.570
'Cause I did have a sort
of technical ability.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:14.570
So I mean every person alive
is a unique individual,

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:16.197
one of a kind.

00:19:16.197 --> 00:19:20.320
If you're listening to this,
there isn't anyone like you.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:24.110
So the fact that there's no one like you,

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:25.370
do what you love

00:19:26.603 --> 00:19:29.542
because that's telling you
what you might be good at.

00:19:29.542 --> 00:19:33.484
And anyway even if you're not
as good as it is you wanna be,

00:19:33.484 --> 00:19:35.870
you're doing something that
you love doing, that's,

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:37.450
you know, they say if
you do what you love,

00:19:37.450 --> 00:19:40.320
you never do a day of work in your life.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:41.540
And it's true.

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:43.610
You know, I would do what I do.

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:45.910
I don't even know, so most of the time

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:47.793
I don't get paid for what I do.

00:19:48.724 --> 00:19:51.000
I basically am doing it
because I would do it

00:19:52.327 --> 00:19:53.224
no matter what.

00:19:53.224 --> 00:19:55.240
And that's what I think I
would tell young people.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:59.070
I remember my daughter, Sophia,
when she was a young girl,

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:03.880
she was a very interesting,
little precocious young lady

00:20:03.880 --> 00:20:07.370
and she always admired fashion,
and she was a good painter.

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:10.723
And she was going to painting
school studying painting,

00:20:11.945 --> 00:20:15.087
and she said to me once,
"Daddy, am I just a dilettante?

00:20:15.087 --> 00:20:17.947
"Am I just a girl, I'm
gonna be studying painting,

00:20:17.947 --> 00:20:21.517
"but I'll never probably
be really a great painter.

00:20:21.517 --> 00:20:25.823
"I like fashion, I like
photography, I like stories.

00:20:26.727 --> 00:20:27.570
"What should I do?"

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:29.370
And I said, "Do everything you love!

00:20:31.485 --> 00:20:32.967
"Why, because if you do
what you love you're going

00:20:32.967 --> 00:20:34.517
"to learn about all the things

00:20:34.517 --> 00:20:36.647
"you love and who knows
what you're gonna be.

00:20:36.647 --> 00:20:39.527
"In five years you may
decide to be something,

00:20:39.527 --> 00:20:42.007
"but if you were doing
the things you love,

00:20:42.007 --> 00:20:44.470
"I bet you that they will be useful."

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:46.580
And then four or five years later she made

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:47.710
a little short film.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:50.670
You can see it on the net
it's called "Like the Star,"

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:53.510
and she was just with
her high school friends.

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:55.694
And you could see when you made that

00:20:55.694 --> 00:20:58.820
that she was a born movie
director and all of her interests,

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:02.570
fashion, photography,
storytelling, painting,

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:05.938
all helped her be a film director.

00:21:05.938 --> 00:21:09.320
So even if you're not sure what
your profession is gonna be,

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:13.320
still do the things you love
because there is no doubt

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:14.890
that if you do those things,

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:18.000
they will be useful when
it finally is clear to you

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:20.700
what your profession that you wish is.

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:24.950
And then you'll have the
benefit of all of the things

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:26.103
you were interested in.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:29.790
- That's super powerful.

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:30.660
You know, the question from Facebook here.

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:33.477
Ginny Nicole says, "Did you
have a person in your life

00:21:33.477 --> 00:21:35.620
"that helped you believe you
can do anything you want?"

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:38.627
It sounded like Sophia had
you and probably others.

00:21:38.627 --> 00:21:41.461
"As a teacher," so Ginny is a teacher,

00:21:41.461 --> 00:21:43.720
"as a teacher, I strive to
make sure I tell each student."

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:45.660
So yeah, who in your
own life and, actually,

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:47.400
maybe in other people's lives

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.063
have you played that role?

00:21:49.063 --> 00:21:50.203
'Cause I know there have been several.

00:21:51.241 --> 00:21:53.100
- Well, when I was a very bad student.

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:56.480
I should have defended
myself, because for my father

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:59.230
getting good grades and
doing well in school,

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:02.450
he had come from a neighborhood
in the sort of area

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:06.970
in New York where the boys
either became hoodlums,

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:11.720
or professional musicians,
or doctors and lawyers.

00:22:11.720 --> 00:22:15.610
There was a split, so he
hated a hoodlum mentality,

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:17.920
someone who didn't do well in school.

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:20.270
But at the same time he
took me out of school

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:23.830
every six months and I was
always in a different school.

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:27.580
Sometimes even in a far distant
area, like I would be going

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:30.810
to school in New York and
then suddenly I'm going

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:33.490
to junior high school in California.

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:36.230
Well, California and New
York was as different,

00:22:36.230 --> 00:22:39.350
more different than any two
places on earth is today.

00:22:39.350 --> 00:22:42.770
I remember being ridiculed
in school in New York,

00:22:42.770 --> 00:22:45.050
because I was eating an avocado,

00:22:45.050 --> 00:22:47.800
because people in New York
had never seen an avocado.

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:50.680
That's how different it was.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:55.573
So my feeling is that, you know, once,

00:22:56.420 --> 00:22:59.080
so I was not in good with my family.

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:01.170
My brother was a brilliant student

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.500
and I was a terrible student.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.700
But my mother did something good for me.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:08.167
My father didn't take me
too seriously when I said,

00:23:08.167 --> 00:23:11.750
"Oh, I wanna be a director
and stuff of plays."

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:14.257
But my mother said, "Listen,
if you're interested in being

00:23:14.257 --> 00:23:18.257
"a director, I'll give you
enough money that you can go

00:23:18.257 --> 00:23:19.130
"to New York."

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:21.277
We lived on the outskirts of New York.

00:23:21.277 --> 00:23:25.810
"I'll let you go to a matinee
of a play every week."

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:28.800
And so every Wednesday I
would go in the subway,

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:32.433
or whatever the method where
I was living to the city,

00:23:33.341 --> 00:23:35.050
and I would see a Broadway
matinee, you know,

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:37.460
which probably cost $8
that was a lot of money,

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:39.770
but she gave me the money to do that,

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:43.550
and I think, you know,
that was a wonderful thing,

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:45.040
a gift she gave me.

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:50.040
Also in UCLA I met a professor
who was one of the only women

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:53.730
movie directors Hollywood ever had,

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:57.918
her name was, this is
funny, Dorothy Arzner,

00:23:57.918 --> 00:24:02.870
and she directed many, many,
many big Hollywood films

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:07.870
with great stars like Katharine
Hepburn and Joan Crawford.

00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:11.970
And she was a major Hollywood
director and the only woman

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:16.970
for many years who was, and
she was very encouraging to me.

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:22.260
There was a time, I can
remember one night, I was broke,

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:26.840
I was miserable, I was lonely, you know.

00:24:28.623 --> 00:24:33.623
And she came out of the bungalow
that where the film school

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:38.587
was in UCLA, and she said to me, she said,

00:24:38.587 --> 00:24:39.727
"You know, you'll make it.

00:24:39.727 --> 00:24:42.860
"I've been around a long
time and I know you will."

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:44.973
And she left, and I was ready to quit,

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:48.683
but that word of this one teacher.

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:55.327
I said, "Wow, she was a great
movie director and if she says

00:24:55.327 --> 00:24:59.050
"that I'm gonna make it,
God maybe I shouldn't,

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:01.553
I wanted to quit and go
get back to New York,

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:04.570
and maybe see if I could
get a job as a stage manager

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:08.870
or something, but that
little voice of encouragement

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:09.763
helped me a lot.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:14.450
I think one thing in the second
part of your question, Sal,

00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:19.110
is that American film
directors have been very good

00:25:19.110 --> 00:25:22.840
about supporting each other
and helping each other.

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:26.680
I mentioned Steven Spielberg,
he has sponsored so many

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:30.320
young directors and made it
possible for them to have

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:35.320
their careers and even I and
my career took a very bright

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:39.423
much younger student whom I had met,

00:25:40.512 --> 00:25:42.940
and, you know, wanted to work with,

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:45.897
I thought he was so bright and I said,

00:25:45.897 --> 00:25:48.920
"I'm gonna try to help you
get to make your first film."

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:50.940
And that was George Lucas who, of course,

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:53.210
made "Star Wars" and "American Graffiti"

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:56.860
and as you know is a legend.

00:25:56.860 --> 00:26:00.639
But he was the skinny 16, about,

00:26:00.639 --> 00:26:02.850
gee how old was he when I met him?

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:04.623
Maybe just 20 or so,

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:08.900
and became, you know,
at first this assistant,

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:12.463
but very quickly not an
assistant, more of an associate.

00:26:13.369 --> 00:26:18.369
And there have been opportunities
to give a helping hand

00:26:18.810 --> 00:26:21.800
or encourage, even the great,

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:24.490
one of the great all-time directors today,

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:27.450
probably the greatest
living working director,

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:30.370
Martin Scorsese, was a kid,

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:34.150
a younger director that
I tried to be helpful to.

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:38.839
I remember I got him the
job to do the film he made

00:26:38.839 --> 00:26:39.807
the film he made with Ellen Burstyn,

00:26:39.807 --> 00:26:42.478
"Alice Doesn't Live Here Anymore."

00:26:42.478 --> 00:26:46.778
So it's part of, it's like being a tree,

00:26:46.778 --> 00:26:49.610
and if you're not planting
new young trees around you,

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:50.853
there won't be a forest.

00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:54.180
- And what did you see
in a young George Lucas

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:55.280
or Martin Scorsese?

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:58.300
I'm guessing that there were
other, I don't know exactly

00:26:58.300 --> 00:26:59.840
where you were in your
career arc, but I'm guessing

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:01.410
by that point you were
starting to get relatively

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:02.860
established or known.

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:04.450
What did you see in them,

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:06.110
probably there were other
people who would love

00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:08.480
mentorship from you, what
spark did you see in them

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:09.988
that said, "Yeah, I think they're gonna

00:27:09.988 --> 00:27:10.821
turn into something."

00:27:11.908 --> 00:27:15.750
- If you scratch a
filmmaker, what comes out is,

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:18.700
no matter how old they are, no matter,

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:22.510
if you scratch the skin a
little, out pours enthusiasm.

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:25.910
To hear George Lucas talk about film,

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:29.400
he would get so excited about,
you know, you take that shot

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:31.810
and you put that together with
that shot and then you get

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:34.480
something that you didn't
imagine could happen.

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:39.230
There's such an outpouring
of love and enthusiasm

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:40.500
for the cinema.

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:44.500
To this day Martin Scorsese
is the best film teacher

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:45.890
I think on earth.

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:49.820
I would recommend very seriously
anyone interested in movies

00:27:49.820 --> 00:27:53.620
to get a documentary
series that Martin made,

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:58.620
called "My Italian Voyage,"

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:02.470
which is a multiset, in which he discusses

00:28:02.470 --> 00:28:03.740
from the beginning to end,

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:07.030
the Italian cinema and
goes into every director

00:28:07.030 --> 00:28:08.250
and what they did.

00:28:08.250 --> 00:28:10.610
You come out after seeing that,

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:14.140
A, you know so much about how the cinema

00:28:14.140 --> 00:28:15.770
evolved and developed,

00:28:15.770 --> 00:28:18.490
but more important, you
just want to make a film.

00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:22.050
And that's great, you know,
that's what young filmmaker,

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:25.390
you know, when I was a
young theater student,

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:29.010
I had three heroes that I
just, I mean they were like

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:32.143
untouchables, it was of course Elia Kazan,

00:28:33.500 --> 00:28:36.150
it was Marlon Brando
and Tennessee Williams.

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:40.293
And they represented to a
theater student of the '50s,

00:28:41.190 --> 00:28:42.740
you know, the ultimate.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:46.300
And of course, I was
blessed to have been able

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:49.100
to meet each of them.

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:52.160
But it's good to have a hero of someone

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:54.330
who, if you're a writer,
of whose books you

00:28:54.330 --> 00:28:57.510
just admire so much or, if it's a dance,

00:28:57.510 --> 00:28:59.390
a dancer who inspires you,

00:28:59.390 --> 00:29:03.820
or a composer, or whoever
it is, a songwriter,

00:29:03.820 --> 00:29:06.700
Carole King, whoever it is you love.

00:29:06.700 --> 00:29:09.430
And it's alright to even imitate them,

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:12.790
because you can't imitate
and that's the beauty of it.

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:15.410
In other words you could
try to write a song

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:18.090
like Carole King, but it goes through you

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:21.500
and it comes out different,
and that's what you want.

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:25.270
I remember once reading
a note about Balzac,

00:29:25.270 --> 00:29:27.577
the great French writer, and someone said,

00:29:27.577 --> 00:29:31.500
"Oh, the young writers
are taking your writing

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:32.720
and they're copying it."

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:35.707
And Balzac said, "Oh I want
them to, I want them to.

00:29:35.707 --> 00:29:38.407
"That's why I do it,
because when they take it,

00:29:38.407 --> 00:29:40.378
"they make it their own,

00:29:40.378 --> 00:29:42.478
"it means I'll live through them forever."

00:29:44.848 --> 00:29:48.920
And this idea that oh, of
course, plagiarism is wrong

00:29:48.920 --> 00:29:50.780
when you take something someone else wrote

00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:51.940
and say you wrote it.

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:55.750
But to be inspired and try to
do something like the person

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:58.690
who inspires you, whether
it's anything music,

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:02.940
film, dance, that's okay,
because you can't just copy it.

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:07.250
In you, you are such a
unique creation yourself

00:30:07.250 --> 00:30:09.790
that it'll come out differently
and little by little

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:12.590
you'll start to understand the difference,

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:16.640
and as it becomes clear to
you that becomes your voice,

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:19.440
and you go on and it becomes
more and more beautiful,

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:22.310
and then some young person
will steal from you,

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:23.850
and you want them to.

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:24.913
It's there for them.

00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:28.970
- That's super powerful
and you know one thing

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:31.490
that I think it comes through
in even just your narrative

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:32.840
and what you see in others,

00:30:33.722 --> 00:30:36.025
but you know the spark, this curiosity,

00:30:36.025 --> 00:30:38.052
I don't think probably the
world fully appreciates

00:30:38.052 --> 00:30:39.430
just how much you have that in you.

00:30:39.430 --> 00:30:41.463
For context, you know, the first time,

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:45.370
well, the last time we
saw each other pre-COVID

00:30:46.552 --> 00:30:47.760
you said, "Yeah, I would love to meet,

00:30:47.760 --> 00:30:49.812
but you've got to teach me calculus."

00:30:49.812 --> 00:30:52.570
(laughs) Tell us a little bit
about your love for learning.

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:55.990
I think you're kind of infectious
when you talk about that.

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:58.460
- Well, that's very
important, because I think

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:03.460
if I could give this
the subject of education

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:06.652
my two cents, be it the teachers,

00:31:06.652 --> 00:31:10.770
the wonderful teachers and
less wonderful teachers,

00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:12.940
the students, the wonderful students,

00:31:12.940 --> 00:31:14.670
the less wonderful students.

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:17.120
What I would say to
them that I have learned

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:20.570
is that the secret to why we wanna learn

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:22.790
is because it's pleasure!

00:31:22.790 --> 00:31:26.963
That learning is one of the
greatest pleasures you can have.

00:31:28.083 --> 00:31:31.470
And if the students could understand that,

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:32.913
sure there's lots of reason you learn

00:31:32.913 --> 00:31:35.600
because then you'll become
more wise and you'll be able to

00:31:35.600 --> 00:31:37.620
get a profession or you'll be a doctor,

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:39.120
or you'll make your parents proud,

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:41.653
or you'll be able to
make money, huh, money.

00:31:42.870 --> 00:31:43.993
All those things.

00:31:44.938 --> 00:31:46.240
The real reason to wanna learn is,

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:49.870
because the more you learn
and learn how to learn,

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:51.970
the more pleasure you get.

00:31:51.970 --> 00:31:54.560
And I always had an example,
I think I even have, yeah,

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:56.463
have it here, why is that true?

00:31:57.300 --> 00:31:59.970
You know because you
think of human beings,

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:03.290
they were born in the mud, you know,

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:05.070
we suddenly saw, where are we?

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:06.310
What is this, where am I?

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:08.590
Is gonna get, it's cold,
I'm cold, I'm hungry.

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:10.140
I better find something to eat.

00:32:11.239 --> 00:32:14.350
So human beings just ran
into a bunch of puzzles

00:32:14.350 --> 00:32:16.320
that they didn't know the answers to.

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:18.690
But because we're gifted
with this wonderful

00:32:18.690 --> 00:32:22.140
ability to think and
the tradition to think

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:24.450
and ultimately we learned how to think

00:32:25.787 --> 00:32:27.880
that we're able to solve the puzzles

00:32:27.880 --> 00:32:30.800
and it's a great pleasure
to solve puzzles.

00:32:30.800 --> 00:32:32.690
And I always make this, I have it here,

00:32:32.690 --> 00:32:34.220
I have this example.

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:36.760
All the kids know this puzzle.

00:32:36.760 --> 00:32:41.160
This is the two nails
that, yeah, go like that

00:32:41.160 --> 00:32:43.860
and if you have one of these,
you say well how do you get

00:32:43.860 --> 00:32:45.183
these two nails apart?

00:32:46.205 --> 00:32:47.990
And some kids can just
figure it out immediately

00:32:47.990 --> 00:32:49.210
and others don't.

00:32:49.210 --> 00:32:51.670
But if someone says,
look, the way to do it

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:55.880
is you line it up, so
that you see how my, yeah.

00:32:55.880 --> 00:32:59.550
You line it up so it's like
this and it's pointing up,

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:01.113
and then you go like that.

00:33:02.199 --> 00:33:04.350
And hope that this is gonna work.

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:08.373
You go like this, twist it around.

00:33:09.949 --> 00:33:12.153
I knew how to do this, wait a second.

00:33:13.430 --> 00:33:14.893
My whole point is being,

00:33:16.700 --> 00:33:17.960
you line them up like that

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:20.373
and then you twist it around like that.

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:24.273
Sal, I failed.

00:33:25.390 --> 00:33:29.400
- This shows a learning process,
we're all on the journey.

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:32.890
- You do it like that and
then you go around like that.

00:33:32.890 --> 00:33:35.760
Or is it around like that?

00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:37.090
Or no, it's up like that.

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:40.113
The kids are laughing at me
because they know how to do it.

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:46.933
Anyway, you put it up like that.

00:33:48.788 --> 00:33:50.038
Then you take one around.

00:33:51.720 --> 00:33:52.883
I'm so close!

00:33:54.210 --> 00:33:56.030
Anyway it's great pleasure to learn

00:33:56.030 --> 00:34:00.220
how to solve this puzzle,
and later I'll do it again.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:03.360
But the learning how to do anything,

00:34:03.360 --> 00:34:05.080
my point is, it's a pleasure.

00:34:05.080 --> 00:34:08.440
If I had been able to teach you
how to do this stupid puzzle

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:11.580
I would have been happy, it
would have given me pleasure.

00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:13.970
But learning everything is a lot of fun,

00:34:13.970 --> 00:34:16.820
and that's the reason
why we we go to school

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:20.790
and we learn is not because
of all this, the sub reasons

00:34:20.790 --> 00:34:24.053
that they tell us, it's because
it's a lot of fun to do.

00:34:26.270 --> 00:34:29.867
- No, kudos to you Francis,
'cause I think, you know,

00:34:29.867 --> 00:34:32.550
in real time on live, I
guess, livestream to try it,

00:34:32.550 --> 00:34:36.130
shows a growth mindset,
not a fear of failure.

00:34:36.130 --> 00:34:38.290
And I think we all want to go and try to--

00:34:38.290 --> 00:34:40.880
- You can't have a fear, you
can't have a fear of failure.

00:34:40.880 --> 00:34:43.665
If you have a fear of failure,
you wouldn't do anything.

00:34:43.665 --> 00:34:44.577
- That's right.

00:34:44.577 --> 00:34:48.780
- And also a failure is
one of the steps of success

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:52.010
because when you learn what not to do,

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:54.530
then you don't do that
again and you skip that.

00:34:54.530 --> 00:34:57.370
So that's the equal step towards success.

00:34:57.370 --> 00:35:00.070
If you make a movie that's a bad movie,

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:03.220
that doesn't work, that
doesn't uplift the audience

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:06.680
or engross them, then
you think about it a lot,

00:35:06.680 --> 00:35:10.117
and you say, "Well,
next time I'm not gonna

00:35:10.117 --> 00:35:11.510
"make those mistakes."

00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:14.880
Someone tricked this, they
made it so I can't do it.

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:17.983
I know, one of my kids, no doubt.

00:35:19.080 --> 00:35:21.690
- They bent the nails
a little bit too much.

00:35:21.690 --> 00:35:24.233
You know, I would be
remiss, I'm getting a lot,

00:35:25.518 --> 00:35:26.610
there's a lot of questions
from a lot of film buffs here

00:35:26.610 --> 00:35:27.960
as you could imagine--

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:30.037
- Okay, I'll try to be less
talkative and I'll try--

00:35:32.525 --> 00:35:33.880
- No, no, this is wonderful!

00:35:33.880 --> 00:35:36.650
No, this is, and keep
solving the nail puzzle.

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:39.420
You know, I mean there's
a lot of questions about,

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:42.022
I mean people are asking what's
your favorite horror films,

00:35:42.022 --> 00:35:43.920
people are asking about some
of the things that, you know,

00:35:43.920 --> 00:35:47.680
Gene Ham from Facebook asks,
you know, Michael Corleone

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:50.347
pops the hole in the orange
and sucks the juice out of it,

00:35:50.347 --> 00:35:52.990
and it's kind of a metaphor
for how he treats life.

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:54.270
Is that stuff you came up with?

00:35:54.270 --> 00:35:55.540
Was it in the script?

00:35:55.540 --> 00:35:57.653
Was it something the actor came up with?

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:00.493
I'd love your views on that.

00:36:03.883 --> 00:36:05.583
Oh, you got it out, oh, very good!

00:36:07.981 --> 00:36:10.587
- You know, the metaphor of
the orange in "The Godfather,"

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:16.427
you know, I basically
saw the oranges around,

00:36:16.427 --> 00:36:18.688
I didn't have an idea about
this could be a symbol

00:36:18.688 --> 00:36:20.750
of anything, I just say, "Hey,
take the orange," you know.

00:36:20.750 --> 00:36:24.130
Or like when I put the cat
in Marlon Brando's hand,

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:26.480
there seemed to be an opportunity

00:36:28.584 --> 00:36:32.570
to use what was around and after a while,

00:36:32.570 --> 00:36:36.720
then when I edited the movie,
I began to understand that

00:36:36.720 --> 00:36:39.200
that the orange said something.

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:42.150
A lot of times when you're
working on something,

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:45.020
you're sort of half in a
trance and you're doing it

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:48.800
because it feels right to you
or it seems to be logical.

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:51.410
Although I do have a rule
when I'm making the film,

00:36:51.410 --> 00:36:54.630
I always try to know
what the overall theme is

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:55.670
in one sentence.

00:36:55.670 --> 00:36:58.823
Like in "The Godfather"
the theme was succession.

00:37:00.528 --> 00:37:03.460
In "Apocalypse Now"
the theme was morality.

00:37:03.460 --> 00:37:06.230
So if I know what the big picture is,

00:37:06.230 --> 00:37:08.930
when they ask me a lot of
questions of well, you know,

00:37:08.930 --> 00:37:11.040
a director just answers questions all day.

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:13.270
Should she be in a dress or in slacks?

00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:14.810
Should she have long hair or short hair?

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:18.150
Should she drive a sports car or a sedan?

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:20.350
And you just say off the top of your head,

00:37:20.350 --> 00:37:22.790
yeah, long hair, yeah,
a dress, or something.

00:37:22.790 --> 00:37:25.420
But when you don't know how to answer it,

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:27.850
then I always say,
"Well, what's the theme?"

00:37:27.850 --> 00:37:30.760
And then, for example, the
movie I made "The Conversation,"

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:33.570
they were asking me what
coat he should wear,

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:36.287
and I said, "Well, he's
really not a detective,

00:37:36.287 --> 00:37:39.020
"so it shouldn't be like
a Humphrey Bogart coat."

00:37:39.020 --> 00:37:41.710
And one of the coats they
showed me was a raincoat

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:44.109
and was transparent, and
I said, well, the theme

00:37:44.109 --> 00:37:46.910
of "The Conversation" is privacy,

00:37:46.910 --> 00:37:49.260
so let me give him a transparent raincoat.

00:37:49.260 --> 00:37:51.990
Sometimes if you don't know the decision,

00:37:51.990 --> 00:37:53.910
if you ask yourself what the theme is,

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:56.480
it will suggest what
the decision should be.

00:37:56.480 --> 00:37:59.263
So there are lots of
tricks that you can learn.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.737
- That's super powerful, you know, we--

00:38:02.737 --> 00:38:05.730
- And my favorite horror film of all time,

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:08.670
oh gee, you know, when
you get to the subject

00:38:08.670 --> 00:38:11.953
of favorite films, there's
always people who come up with

00:38:11.953 --> 00:38:12.786
oh, my 10 favorite films.

00:38:12.786 --> 00:38:14.170
That is so absurd!

00:38:14.170 --> 00:38:16.520
You can come up with a
thousand favorite films

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:20.520
because the cinema, which has
only existed for 140 years,

00:38:20.520 --> 00:38:24.340
was blessed with the most
wonderful masterpieces,

00:38:24.340 --> 00:38:26.960
even in the '30s, when movies were silent,

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:31.133
there were already 10,
20, 30 masterpieces.

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:34.970
So when you ask what is
your favorite horror film,

00:38:34.970 --> 00:38:37.470
you could say "Nosferatu" of Murnau,

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:38.780
which was a silent film.

00:38:38.780 --> 00:38:41.900
It's a silent version of
a kind of Dracula story,

00:38:41.900 --> 00:38:43.180
that's a great one.

00:38:43.180 --> 00:38:45.137
Or you could say "Alien,"

00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:49.543
that was unbelievably scary and horrible.

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:54.070
What other, there are
probably four or five really.

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:56.881
Or you could talk of Japanese films,

00:38:56.881 --> 00:39:00.290
the film of Mizoguchi "Ugetsu."

00:39:00.290 --> 00:39:02.300
That's really beautiful.

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:07.240
So cinema is so filled with
a bounty that you can just

00:39:07.240 --> 00:39:11.180
enjoy for your whole lifetime,
seeing the great works

00:39:11.180 --> 00:39:13.150
that were made and they tend to make you

00:39:13.150 --> 00:39:16.270
want to make one too, because it seems

00:39:16.270 --> 00:39:18.660
like such a wonderful feel, the cinema,

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:22.970
it's truly one of the,
well, all the arts are.

00:39:22.970 --> 00:39:26.330
And I, of course, like
to encourage young people

00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:29.950
to consider going in the
arts because their parents

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:31.510
they just say, oh, become an accountant,

00:39:31.510 --> 00:39:33.960
or become a lawyer, or become a doctor,

00:39:33.960 --> 00:39:37.250
but partly is because their
parents want them to be able

00:39:37.250 --> 00:39:40.350
to make a living, and the
truth of the matter is

00:39:40.350 --> 00:39:42.970
the arts is a wonderful
way to make a living,

00:39:42.970 --> 00:39:46.310
because what they call
today intellectual property,

00:39:46.310 --> 00:39:49.630
which is when you make a
book or you make a painting,

00:39:49.630 --> 00:39:52.440
and stuff that's very valuable,

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:55.150
because it's such a beautiful thing.

00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:58.733
So I think young people
should consider being artists.

00:39:59.601 --> 00:40:01.240
It's A, you will love doing it,

00:40:01.240 --> 00:40:05.750
B, you will be making other
people happy and giving them

00:40:05.750 --> 00:40:08.480
something that will enrich their lives.

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:13.060
And I think it's one of the
great professions to choose.

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:15.140
You know, more and more
as we go into the future,

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:17.213
there's going to be less and less jobs.

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:20.820
This is not necessarily
bad, which it means is that

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:23.900
people are gonna be able
to do more what they love,

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:27.833
rather than the toil part, you
know, the work that you hate.

00:40:28.690 --> 00:40:32.890
Machines, and AI, and other
things are gonna do that.

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:34.560
And you say, "What about the money?"

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:38.590
And eventually, you know,
I believe, this is crazy,

00:40:38.590 --> 00:40:40.860
but just like today we all pay taxes,

00:40:40.860 --> 00:40:44.440
in the future the state will give you,

00:40:44.440 --> 00:40:45.740
will pay you the taxes

00:40:45.740 --> 00:40:49.370
because you are a citizen
and you're entitled,

00:40:49.370 --> 00:40:51.330
because more and more non-living,

00:40:51.330 --> 00:40:54.010
what Aristotle called non-living machines,

00:40:54.010 --> 00:40:56.020
are gonna be doing the work, the toil.

00:40:56.020 --> 00:40:58.223
I shouldn't call it work, work is fun.

00:40:59.920 --> 00:41:03.040
- Right, and one last, I mean
that we could talk for hours,

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:04.120
I know we're already over time,

00:41:04.120 --> 00:41:05.680
but maybe just a good capstone.

00:41:05.680 --> 00:41:07.000
There's a lot of young people watching,

00:41:07.000 --> 00:41:09.200
it is around the graduation time.

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:10.790
You know, what advice
would you give to them?

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:13.810
Or another way to think about
it, if you were them today,

00:41:13.810 --> 00:41:18.810
what would you want to work
on, what would you wanna know?

00:41:20.690 --> 00:41:24.110
- Well, I think this is gonna,
I know it's a tough time

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:26.120
and people are scared about the future.

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:30.110
I see the future, even the
pandemic, as teaching us

00:41:30.110 --> 00:41:33.110
some good lessons that we should value,

00:41:33.110 --> 00:41:34.800
as to what's really important.

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:37.493
If you ask people today,
what's really important?

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:41.270
It's that my family is okay,

00:41:41.270 --> 00:41:43.400
that the people I love are okay,

00:41:43.400 --> 00:41:45.170
that my friends are okay,

00:41:47.860 --> 00:41:50.040
it's less "Oh, I'm
gonna be a billionaire."

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:52.670
I have a new word, by the
way, better than billionaire.

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:54.830
I call it sufficientaire.

00:41:54.830 --> 00:41:58.990
A sufficientaire, having enough
for what you need, you know,

00:41:58.990 --> 00:42:02.400
you don't want to just pile
up a lot of excess stuff.

00:42:02.400 --> 00:42:06.370
I can't believe that the
so-called 1% that they talk about

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:09.860
that have all those hundreds
of billions of dollars,

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:12.990
I can't believe that
they're any happier than

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:14.583
some really happy person.

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:17.930
You know, thinking you're gonna have money

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:21.960
makes you happy, anticipating
having money makes you happy,

00:42:21.960 --> 00:42:23.600
having it is a whole...

00:42:23.600 --> 00:42:25.780
Those people are miserable, you know,

00:42:25.780 --> 00:42:27.840
because all they're doing is managing,

00:42:27.840 --> 00:42:29.620
worrying about losing it and stuff.

00:42:29.620 --> 00:42:30.730
That's not happiness.

00:42:30.730 --> 00:42:32.830
That's not even a smart thing to do.

00:42:32.830 --> 00:42:35.450
The smart thing to do is
have a lot of friends,

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:38.440
have a lot of capability,
being able to do things,

00:42:38.440 --> 00:42:41.400
and having the freedom
to be able to do it,

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:43.023
having a great education.

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:47.440
I think that we should
be educating ourselves,

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:50.660
go to school or being
involved in some school thing

00:42:50.660 --> 00:42:52.113
for our entire lives.

00:42:53.013 --> 00:42:55.180
I do it, I'm am 81 and learning is

00:42:55.180 --> 00:42:57.290
my greatest source of pleasure.

00:42:57.290 --> 00:43:01.750
I'm reading a book right now
about fungi that, I tell you,

00:43:01.750 --> 00:43:04.380
I always take good notes when
I read because I may want

00:43:04.380 --> 00:43:07.170
to remember what, I read
so many books, you know,

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:09.400
you forget what you learn,
so I take good notes.

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:13.890
I can barely get through
two paragraphs of this book

00:43:13.890 --> 00:43:15.750
without underlining it.

00:43:15.750 --> 00:43:18.510
I'm underlining the whole
book 'cause when you talk

00:43:18.510 --> 00:43:21.840
about the subject of
fungi which, of course,

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:25.120
I think I forget what that ism is called,

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:27.020
when you study that, it is so amazing.

00:43:28.099 --> 00:43:29.860
That is so different than we think

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:34.172
of how these networks
work and how they think.

00:43:34.172 --> 00:43:39.110
It is just utterly a
joy to learn something.

00:43:39.110 --> 00:43:41.110
Every time at night when I go to bed,

00:43:41.110 --> 00:43:42.940
I always read before I fall asleep

00:43:42.940 --> 00:43:45.040
and I always learn so much,

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:47.520
and that's my real pleasure
of life is learning.

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:49.830
And I would tell the young person,

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:53.320
you never stop learning,
never stop studying,

00:43:53.320 --> 00:43:55.723
always be learning more and more,

00:43:56.617 --> 00:43:59.317
not because it's useful, but
because it's pleasurable.

00:44:00.740 --> 00:44:04.240
- I love that, and as I consider myself

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:05.870
a very lucky sufficientaire.

00:44:05.870 --> 00:44:08.510
So from one sufficientaire to another,

00:44:08.510 --> 00:44:10.870
thank you so much, Francis.

00:44:10.870 --> 00:44:13.720
We're the sufficientaire
club, which is an honor

00:44:13.720 --> 00:44:15.870
because not everyone
has sufficientairness.

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:18.191
- No, no, definitely.

00:44:18.191 --> 00:44:20.780
But if more people were
content to be sufficientaires

00:44:20.780 --> 00:44:22.630
instead of billionaires,

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:24.800
then we could all be sufficientaires.

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:27.599
And that's the goal.
- Right, right.

00:44:27.599 --> 00:44:28.450
That's powerful.

00:44:28.450 --> 00:44:30.840
- And we wouldn't lose
all the individuality,

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:32.523
I mean there was still, you know,

00:44:34.369 --> 00:44:37.264
like I made a dumb remark at people who,

00:44:37.264 --> 00:44:39.080
I didn't actually make the remark,

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:41.820
I was asked at a press conference
if I agreed with something

00:44:41.820 --> 00:44:44.370
one of my colleagues
said that Marvel pictures

00:44:44.370 --> 00:44:47.610
were sort of not real cinema.

00:44:47.610 --> 00:44:49.851
And I said, of course, they're cinema.

00:44:49.851 --> 00:44:53.621
Some of them are just made
by wonderful talented people

00:44:53.621 --> 00:44:57.080
and the ingenuity they
show, but the problem is,

00:44:57.080 --> 00:44:58.223
is what the goal?

00:44:59.862 --> 00:45:02.940
And in the economics that
I read there's the idea

00:45:03.784 --> 00:45:05.910
that if you make a lot
of money doing things

00:45:05.910 --> 00:45:09.910
that are good for humanity,
that's just, that it's good.

00:45:09.910 --> 00:45:13.640
Like if you did something
that just gave people

00:45:13.640 --> 00:45:15.313
a lot of pleasure or everything,

00:45:16.258 --> 00:45:17.860
you're entitled to to be rewarded.

00:45:17.860 --> 00:45:20.840
But to make money that
doesn't contribute anything

00:45:20.840 --> 00:45:22.590
is despicable, you know.

00:45:22.590 --> 00:45:25.480
I said that not about Marvel pictures,

00:45:25.480 --> 00:45:28.090
I said it about just
anyone who does anything

00:45:28.090 --> 00:45:30.840
that he just makes a lot of
money that doesn't do any good

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:32.620
for anyone except him.

00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:33.860
I said that was despicable.

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:37.230
So the headline was, "Coppola
thinks Marvel Pictures

00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:40.097
are despicable," which I never said at all

00:45:40.097 --> 00:45:41.880
and I don't think it's true.

00:45:41.880 --> 00:45:45.010
But, boy, did I have a
hassle with that one, so.

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:47.270
Because sometimes, you
know, it's the headlines

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:49.950
that argue rather than what is said.

00:45:49.950 --> 00:45:53.230
Any rate, I still do believe that.

00:45:53.230 --> 00:45:57.842
I believe that, you know,
your work if it benefit you,

00:45:57.842 --> 00:46:01.223
if you gain some wealth
from it or some privilege,

00:46:02.619 --> 00:46:05.870
if you, as I said, my friend George Lucas,

00:46:05.870 --> 00:46:09.040
think of how many young
people he made happy

00:46:09.040 --> 00:46:10.590
with the "Star Wars" films,

00:46:10.590 --> 00:46:14.530
what he gave to them and,
besides, those people usually

00:46:14.530 --> 00:46:16.500
when they get a lot of dough then,

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:19.240
like George, he's giving
it all away anyway

00:46:19.240 --> 00:46:24.240
for education and stuff,
and as it should be.

00:46:24.310 --> 00:46:27.159
So yes, if we were all sufficientairs,

00:46:27.159 --> 00:46:28.963
there would be no poverty.

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:33.070
- Well, that's a powerful
statement to end on.

00:46:33.070 --> 00:46:34.540
Well thank you so much, Francis.

00:46:34.540 --> 00:46:35.960
I hope we can do this again.

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:38.510
I'm pretty confident the entire
community that's watching

00:46:38.510 --> 00:46:41.841
would love this to continue
for hours. (laughs)

00:46:41.841 --> 00:46:43.040
But thank you so much.

00:46:43.040 --> 00:46:44.610
- It's my pleasure and my honor.

00:46:44.610 --> 00:46:48.650
And you know, I never spend
any time with students.

00:46:48.650 --> 00:46:51.380
I consider myself a student, as you know,

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:54.390
and I never spent time with them

00:46:54.390 --> 00:46:55.703
and if I could hear them and stuff,

00:46:55.703 --> 00:46:57.950
I'll learned every bit as much from them

00:46:57.950 --> 00:47:00.300
as that they could possibly learn from me.

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:02.590
And my respect to you,

00:47:02.590 --> 00:47:06.780
what you've invented, Sal, is really

00:47:07.810 --> 00:47:09.863
important and wonderful,

00:47:10.824 --> 00:47:12.740
helps so many students get through stuff

00:47:12.740 --> 00:47:14.587
that they think they can't understand,

00:47:14.587 --> 00:47:16.937
and you teach them that
they can understand it.

00:47:18.760 --> 00:47:20.450
- Thank you, appreciate that so much.

00:47:20.450 --> 00:47:23.000
Maybe we will do some videos
together in the near future.

00:47:23.000 --> 00:47:23.833
- Okay.

00:47:25.780 --> 00:47:26.990
Ciao.

00:47:26.990 --> 00:47:28.060
- Ciao.

00:47:28.060 --> 00:47:29.900
Well, thanks everyone for joining us.

00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:32.400
You can tell I went way
over because frankly

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:34.090
it was just a fascinating conversation.

00:47:34.090 --> 00:47:36.679
And I guess we don't
really have a hard stop.

00:47:36.679 --> 00:47:39.453
I hope Francis, I didn't
keep him from something else.

00:47:39.453 --> 00:47:41.780
But as you can tell Francis
is the type of person

00:47:41.780 --> 00:47:44.980
that you wish you could, you
know, have multiple evenings

00:47:44.980 --> 00:47:48.000
chatting about pretty much any topic.

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:49.393
Hopefully you enjoyed that.

00:47:50.260 --> 00:47:52.330
Today is Friday already, so.

00:47:52.330 --> 00:47:55.768
And I'm actually going to be
on a staycation next week.

00:47:55.768 --> 00:47:56.637
You know, people said,

00:47:56.637 --> 00:47:57.600
"Where are you going on your vacation?"

00:47:57.600 --> 00:48:00.050
I'm like, "Mountain View,"
which is where I live.

00:48:01.211 --> 00:48:02.820
(laughs) But so, we won't be having

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:06.280
the livestream next week, but
the week after we'll be back.

00:48:06.280 --> 00:48:08.830
So thanks everyone for
joining in and see you then.

